WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.115
Sometimes the easiest way to clean data is to take things away.

2
00:00:05.115 --> 00:00:10.148
In this lesson, you will learn to remove columns with a simple minus sign,

3
00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:14.822
and to remove rows by their number, one or several at a time.

4
00:00:14.822 --> 00:00:16.620
Let's tidy up our table.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.784
Our demographic table has five columns.

6
00:00:20.784 --> 00:00:26.113
Under the note, to remove a column from a data frame, we use select

7
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:29.920
again, but with a minus sign before the column name.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.597
Demographic, pipe, select, minus, district name. The minus means:

9
00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:46.640
everything except this column.

10
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:01.853
Run it.

11
00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:05.267
In the Environment, demographic now has four variables.

12
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.400
The district column is gone.

13
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.419
Next, I remove the sex of household head column the same way.

14
00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.260
Now we have three variables.

15
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.419
To remove two columns at once, put both inside select, each with a minus

16
00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:50.355
sign, separated by a comma.

17
00:01:50.355 --> 00:01:55.000
First, I recreate the full table, so we can try it again.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:08.903
Now rows.

19
00:02:08.903 --> 00:02:11.611
Rows are removed with square brackets.

20
00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:16.577
Demographic, open bracket, minus one hundred thirty five, comma, close bracket.

21
00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:20.189
The number before the comma is the row.

22
00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:23.800
Nothing after the comma means: keep all columns.

23
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.779
Before running it, let's look at the table.

24
00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.680
The last row is number one hundred thirty five.

25
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.388
Run it, and the Environment shows one hundred thirty four observations.

26
00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:45.180
Row one hundred thirty five is gone.

27
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.058
For several rows, use c inside the brackets:

28
00:03:06.058 --> 00:03:11.027
minus c, one hundred thirty one, one hundred thirty two, and so on.

29
00:03:11.027 --> 00:03:13.320
Be careful to close every bracket.

30
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.464
Minus means remove.

31
00:03:24.464 --> 00:03:26.416
For columns, inside select.

32
00:03:26.416 --> 00:03:30.320
For rows, inside square brackets. You've got this!

